{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And YHWH said to Aaron.</b> Rashi: He said to Moses that he should say to Aaron. Who compelled Rashi to say thus? Rather, this is what the verse says: the judgment is not with them to cry out and say, 'all who approach the mishkan of YHWH will die' — for HaShem has already said concerning Aaron, 'you and your sons,' etc. And in the foreign tongue, 'א״י או״ט די״ט.' And this is what Rashi explained: He said to Moses that he should say to Aaron — meaning, He had already said to Moses in the parashah of Bemidbar that the Levites are to guard against a stranger approaching the sanctuary. And it was repeated here because of something newly added in it: <b>you and your sons and your father's house</b>, etc. And because it is juxtaposed to what follows it, the parashah of Korah who challenged the priesthood, and to what precedes it, the parashah establishing the priestly gifts through a covenant of salt. But one may object to Rashi, for in the Sifrei they expounded regarding 'and YHWH spoke to Aaron': do I hear 'speak to Aaron'? Scripture says, 'a memorial to the children of Israel, so that no stranger approach' — from this we learn that the speaking was to Moses, that he should say to Aaron."], :book :numbers, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {0 "The phrase 'א״י או״ט די״ט' is in Old French (la'az); the abbreviations are not fully resolved here as the Romance words they represent are uncertain."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:18:28.472699515Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}